{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Los Angeles Dodgers arrived as defending World Series champions and spent most of the year reminding the baseball world why. With 106 wins, a 3.01 ERA, and 237 home runs, they were the class of the National League — a deep, relentless roster that ground opponents into dust. Yet the season's highest-leverage moments tell a more complicated story beneath the dominant surface, one defined by spectacular individual flashes, a bullpen that occasionally betrayed them, and a postseason that ended in heartbreak just short of the World Series.\n\nThe early months were a steady, if imperfect, construction project. Max Muncy's three-run blast on Jackie Robinson Day announced the defending champs were ready, but Kenley Jansen's wobbles and a parade of young relievers in over their heads gave April and May an anxious undertone. The offense carried the load — Gavin Lux smashing a walk-off three-run homer in May, Austin Barnes delivering a clutch three-run shot late in the month — and the Dodgers posted 16-11 marks in both April and May, winning enough to stay comfortable without ever looking invincible.\n\nEverything changed in August. The Dodgers went a scorching 21-6, the best month of their season, transforming from a very good team into something fearsome. September was nearly as dominant at 19-7, with Trea Turner — acquired at the trade deadline — providing star power that lifted the entire lineup. The team closed the regular season in cruise control, and when Chris Taylor delivered a walk-off homer to dispatch the Cardinals in the Wild Card Game, it felt like destiny was lining up for a second straight title.\n\nBut the Atlanta Braves had other ideas. The NLCS was a brutal affair. Kenley Jansen's reliability evaporated at the worst moments — allowing back-to-back crushing hits in Game 4 — and Eddie Rosario turned into a postseason legend against Los Angeles pitching, driving in runs in wave after wave. Cody Bellinger's three-run homer in Game 5 gave the Dodgers a fleeting lifeline, but Atlanta's bats, led by the relentless Rosario, ultimately proved too much. The Dodgers' magnificent 106-win season ended one series too soon, a painful reminder that October rewards the hot team, not always the best one.",
